@node	5.3, 5.4, 5.2, Chapter 5
@section Storage Allocation and Garbage Collection

In section @ref{5.4}, we will show how to implement the guest evaluator as a
register machine.  In order to simplify the discussion, we will assume that our
register machines can be equipped with a @newterm{list-structured memory}, in
which the basic operations for manipulating list-structured data are primitive.
Postulating the existence of such a memory is a useful abstraction when one is
focusing on the mechanisms of control in the guest evaluator, but this does
not reflect a realistic view of the actual primitive data operations of
contemporary computers.  To obtain a more complete picture of how the guest runtime operates, we must investigate how list structure can be represented in a way
that is compatible with conventional computer memories.

There are two considerations in implementing list structure.  The first is
purely an issue of representation: how to represent the ``box-and-pointer''
structure of guest pairs, using only the storage and addressing capabilities of
typical computer memories.  The second issue concerns the management of memory
as a computation proceeds.  The operation of an evaluator depends crucially on
the ability to continually create new data objects.  These include objects that
are explicitly created by the guest procedures being interpreted as well as
structures created by the interpreter itself, such as environments and argument
lists.  Although the constant creation of new data objects would pose no
problem on a computer with an infinite amount of rapidly addressable memory,
computer memories are available only in finite sizes (more's the pity).
Runtimes that continually create data objects therefore provide
@newterm{automatic storage allocation} to support the illusion of infinite
memory.  When an object is no longer needed, its memory is recycled for new
objects.  There are various allocation techniques; the one discussed here is
called @newterm{garbage collection}.
In this edition the list-structured memory of @ref{5.3.1} is the class
@code{Memory} in the chapter's source set beside the 5.2 simulator: the two
vectors @code{the-cars} and @code{the-cdrs}, the free pointer, and the
allocation path, with the list-structure operations installed as machine
operations over the simulator, the book's own assumption for this section.

