@noindent
A pointer to a number, such as @code{n4}, might consist of a type indicating
numeric data together with the actual representation of the number
4.@footnote{This decision on the representation of numbers determines whether
@code{eq?}, which tests equality of pointers, can be used to test for equality
of numbers.  If the pointer contains the number itself, then equal numbers will
have the same pointer.  But if the pointer contains the index of a location
where the number is stored, equal numbers will be guaranteed to have equal
pointers only if we are careful never to store the same number in more than one
location.}  To deal with numbers that are too large to be represented in the
fixed amount of space allocated for a single pointer, we could use a distinct
@newterm{bignum} data type, for which the pointer designates a list in which
the parts of the number are stored.@footnote{This is just like writing a number
as a sequence of digits, except that each ``digit'' is a number between 0 and
the largest number that can be stored in a single pointer.}

The original model also discusses interned symbol pointers.  The Kotlin guest
subset has no symbol-valued @code{GValue}, so this memory model represents
pairs, Long integers, and the empty list; textual names in examples are host
strings, not guest symbols.  The historical interning discussion belongs to
the source language's runtime and is not an executable feature of this unit.

@subsubheading Implementing the primitive list operations

Given the representation scheme, each primitive list operation uses the
@code{Memory} object's two arrays and a typed pair pointer.  The methods
@code{car}, @code{cdr}, @code{setCar}, and @code{setCdr} extract the pointer
index and access the corresponding array slot.  Allocation stores both fields
at @code{free} and advances that index.  This edition exposes memory
operations as a @code{Map<String, MachineOp>} for the register machine:

The actual operation map is:

@example kotlin
public fun listOperations(memory: Memory): Map<String,
    MachineOp> =
    mapOf(
        "null?" to @{ args -> GValue.VBool(argAt(args,
            0) is GValue.VNull) @},
        "pair?" to @{ args -> GValue.VBool(isPair(argAt(args,
            0))) @},
        "number?" to @{ args ->
            GValue.VBool(isNumber(argAt(args, 0)))
        @},
        "car" to @{ args -> memory.car(argAt(args, 0)) @},
        "cdr" to @{ args -> memory.cdr(argAt(args, 0)) @},
        "cons" to @{ args -> memory.cons(argAt(args,
            0),
                argAt(args,
            1)) @},
        "set-car!" to @{ args ->
            memory.setCar(argAt(args,
                0),
                    argAt(args,
                1))
            GValue.VUnit
        @},
        "set-cdr!" to @{ args ->
            memory.setCdr(argAt(args,
                0),
                    argAt(args,
                1))
            GValue.VUnit
        @},
    )
@end example

@noindent
For example, we can make a register machine support the instructions

@example kotlin
Assign(⟨@var{reg₁}⟩, Source.OpSrc("car",
    listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₂}⟩))))
Assign(⟨@var{reg₁}⟩, Source.OpSrc("cdr",
    listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₂}⟩))))
@end example

@noindent
if we implement these, respectively, as

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun car(word: GValue): GValue = theCars[pairSlot(word,
    "car")]

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun cdr(word: GValue): GValue = theCdrs[pairSlot(word,
    "cdr")]

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun pairSlot(word: GValue,
    operation: String): Int =
    pairIndexOf(word) ?: r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(NO_POSITION))
@end example

@noindent
The instructions

@example kotlin
Perform(OpAct("set-car!", listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₁}⟩),
    Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₂}⟩))))
Perform(OpAct("set-cdr!", listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₁}⟩),
    Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₂}⟩))))
@end example

@noindent
are implemented as

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun setCar(word: GValue, value: GValue) @{
    theCars[pairSlot(word, "set-car!")] = value
@}

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun setCdr(word: GValue, value: GValue) @{
    theCdrs[pairSlot(word, "set-cdr!")] = value
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Cons} is performed by allocating an unused index and storing the
arguments to @code{cons} in @code{the-cars} and @code{the-cdrs} at that indexed
vector position.  We presume that there is a special register, @code{free},
that always holds a pair pointer containing the next available index, and that
we can increment the index part of that pointer to find the next free
location.@footnote{There are other ways of finding free storage.  For example,
we could link together all the unused pairs into a @newterm{free list}.  Our
free locations are consecutive (and hence can be accessed by incrementing a
pointer) because we are using a compacting garbage collector, as we will see in
@ref{5.3.2}.}  For example, the instruction

@example kotlin
Assign(
    ⟨@var{reg₁}⟩,
    Source.OpSrc("cons", listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₂}⟩),
        Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg₃}⟩))),
)
@end example

@noindent
is implemented as the following sequence of vector operations:@footnote{This is
essentially the implementation of @code{cons} in terms of @code{set-car!} and
@code{set-cdr!}, as described in @ref{3.3.1}.  The operation
@code{get-new-pair} used in that implementation is realized here by the
@code{free} pointer.}

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
public fun Memory.cons(car: GValue,
    cdr: GValue): GValue @{
    if (free >= size) r.raise(GuestError.IndexOutOfBounds(NO_POSITION))
    theCars[free] = car
    theCdrs[free] = cdr
    return pairPointer(free++)
@}
// On fresh memory, cons(VLong(1L), VLong(2L)) returns
// pair p0.
// The cell contains (n1 n2); the free index advances to
// 1.
@end example

@noindent
Equality and the @code{null?}, @code{pair?}, and @code{number?} predicates
are ordinary machine operations over @code{GValue}; the memory module
provides the latter two predicates and stores the empty list as
@code{GValue.VNull}.  It does not provide a symbol predicate, because there
is no symbol-valued @code{GValue} in this guest runtime.

@example kotlin
public fun isPair(word: GValue): Boolean = pairIndexOf(word) != null

public fun isNumber(word: GValue): Boolean = word is GValue.VLong
@end example

@noindent

@subsubheading Implementing stacks

Although our register machines use stacks, we need do nothing special here,
since stacks can be modeled in terms of lists.  The stack can be a list of the
saved values, pointed to by a special register @code{the-stack}.  Thus, 
@code{Save(⟨@var{reg}⟩)} can be implemented as

@example kotlin
Assign(
    "the-stack",
    Source.OpSrc("cons", listOf(Source.RegSrc(⟨@var{reg}⟩),
        Source.RegSrc("the-stack"))),
)
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, @code{Restore(⟨@var{reg}⟩)} can be implemented as

@example kotlin
Assign(⟨@var{reg}⟩, Source.OpSrc("car",
    listOf(Source.RegSrc("the-stack"))))
Assign("the-stack", Source.OpSrc("cdr",
    listOf(Source.RegSrc("the-stack"))))
@end example

@noindent
A list-backed design can represent an empty stack by storing the guest
empty-list value:

@example kotlin
Assign("the-stack", Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VNull))
@end example

@noindent
These operations can be further expanded in terms of the vector operations
given above.  In conventional computer architectures, however, it is usually
advantageous to allocate the stack as a separate vector.  Then pushing and
popping the stack can be accomplished by incrementing or decrementing an index
into that vector. In this edition the machines of the exercises run on the 5.2 simulator,
whose @code{Save} and @code{Restore} already push and pop its monitored
@code{Stack}; the simulated stack is not list structure.  The constructor
snippets above express the book's lower-level design using the typed
@code{Stmt} family.

